vendor/fflate.js is fflate (https://github.com/101arrowz/fflate, MIT), built
and minified by the engine's author and kept in the engine's repository: it is
taken from there as the other files are, and inflates the fonts of a Kindle
book. Its licence is in vendor/LICENSE-fflate, a file of this project: the
engine's repository carries the code without its notice.

Local changes: none.

The project publishes no release, so the commit is the version. To update:
fetch the same files at the new commit, replace them here, change the commit
and the date above, and run the tests in reader-tests/.

What the page works around instead of patching (see ../reader.js):
  - a section that fails to load is noticed by wrapping each section's load();
  - transitional relocations (blank edge pages, the offset left by the previous
    section) are filtered, and bursts are coalesced;
  - the first scroll after a navigation in scrolled flow is not reported by the
    paginator, so the page nudges it by one pixel;
  - a search still being consumed keeps adding its results after a new one
    starts, so ../find.js runs one search at a time and waits for the old one
    to stop (at the end of the section it is matching) before the next begins;
  - footnotes.js cancels a link before it knows whether it leads to a note: a
    missing target on its heuristic path then fails with the link already
    cancelled, and a missing fragment on its certain path shows the whole file.
    ../notes.js follows the link itself in both cases, and also when what would
    be shown is longer than a note;
  - the loader of epub.js counts a second user of a loaded section only once
    (`ref(href)` without a parent), so closing the second note view that
    showed the section on screen revoked the main view's document: ../reader.js
    counts the users of each section itself, next to the wrapped load(), and
    lets the engine load a section for the first and unload it after the last;
  - a paginator closed while its section loads, or before its next frame or
    its fonts, fails on its missing view (destroy(), setStyles(), fonts.ready)
    and keeps the section loaded: ../notes.js keeps a note view it lets go of
    in the page, unseen, until the engine is done with it;
  - a paginator lays its section out again on each change of its size, also
    when the document on screen has no body (its book closed while it loaded),
    and fails there: ../reader.js wraps render() to pass such a document by;
  - a turn locks the paginator, which then drops any other navigation without a
    word: ../reader.js has a move of the host wait for a turn under way;
  - mobi.js opens a Kindle book whose text is encrypted and shows what it
    decodes of it: ../formats.js reads the encryption field of the book's
    first record itself and refuses the book; it also reads past the end of a
    file cut short as if nothing were there, and fb2.js makes a book of what
    the browser could parse of a file that is not well-formed: ../formats.js
    checks the list of records of the one and parses the other first, and
    refuses a book in which nothing can be read;
  - mobi.js gives the documents of a Kindle book their addresses itself, without
    the `data` event epub.js sends, so resource hints cannot be taken out there:
    ../formats.js replaces the book's `serializer`, through which each document
    goes on its way to an address, by one that takes them out first;
  - the sections of mobi.js and fb2.js have no unload(), and some of a KF8 book
    no load(): the wrapping of ../reader.js takes them as they are;
  - a KF8 book resolves a link in its own time (resolveHref() is asynchronous):
    ../reader.js waits for it where it labels the sections. ../notes.js does
    not: a link to a note of such a book is followed, never shown in the panel.

What is left as it is: the engine registers and outlines all the results of a
section before handing them over, so when the cap of ../find.js lands in the
middle of a section or just before one with results, or a search stops while a
section is being matched, that section may be outlined with results the host
was never sent. And when a new book gives up waiting for the old book's search
(after 8 s), that search, resuming late, may leave outlines of the old book in
the new one until the next search there.
